                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p style="text-align: justify;"><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Pharmacology is the discipline that investigates the principles and mechanisms governing interactions between drugs and the organism. This course provides a systematic overview of pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics, as well as foundational knowledge and emerging advances across major drug classes, including mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetic characteristics, clinical applications, and adverse drug reactions. Through didactic instruction, case-based analysis, and hands-on laboratory practice, students will acquire principles of rational drug use and develop a scientific understanding of the logic underlying pharmacotherapy, thereby laying a solid foundation for clinical practice and drug discovery and development.</span></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
